3:40–4:30 — Benefits, trade-offs, and production considerations
⏱️ 3:40–4:30 — BENEFITS, TRADE-OFFS & PRODUCTION CONSIDERATIONS

AREA: 🔗 LOOSE COUPLING
Senior-engineer perspective:
Business workflow depends on the Product interface,
not on a specific implementation.

--------------------------------------------------

AREA: ➕ EXTENSIBILITY
Senior-engineer perspective:
New products can be introduced through new Concrete
Creators when they follow the existing contract.

--------------------------------------------------

AREA: 🛠️ MAINTAINABILITY
Senior-engineer perspective:
The shared workflow stays in one place instead of
being duplicated across multiple implementations.

--------------------------------------------------

AREA: ⚖️ COMPLEXITY
Senior-engineer perspective:
The pattern introduces additional classes and
an inheritance hierarchy.

--------------------------------------------------

AREA: ♻️ OBJECT LIFECYCLE
Senior-engineer perspective:
The pattern alone does not provide dependency
injection, lifecycle management, or resource pooling.

--------------------------------------------------

AREA: 🌐 DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
Senior-engineer perspective:
The pattern does not provide distributed coordination,
retries, idempotency, or delivery guarantees.
===============================================================================================================================================
From a production perspective, :
            I would also evaluate whether inheritance is the right extension mechanism.
            
            In a Spring Boot application, I might inject configured generator implementations into a 
            registry instead of constructing them directly with `new`. 
            This allows Spring to manage dependencies, configuration, and lifecycle.
            
            I would also ensure that the Product interface represents a stable business capability, 
            and that provider-specific concerns such as timeouts, observability, error handling,
            and retries are addressed at the appropriate architectural layer.
            
            The Factory Method pattern solves a specific problem: varying object creation while preserving a common creation workflow. It should not be treated as a complete application architecture.



===============================================================================================================================================
To summarize,
            Factory Method is useful when a common workflow must remain 
            stable while subclasses control which concrete Product gets created.

            Its core participants are the Product interface, Concrete Products, 
            the Creator, and Concrete Creators. The key mechanism is runtime polymorphism through an overridable factory method.

            For senior-level design, I would also distinguish Factory Method from a Simple Factory,
            which typically centralizes selection in a method or class, and from Dependency Injection, 
            which supplies dependencies from outside the consuming object.

            My final design decision would depend on whether creation genuinely varies by Creator, 
            whether a shared workflow exists, and whether the benefits justify the additional abstraction. 
            I would prefer the simplest design that supports the expected evolution of the system.



